  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nHi-Res models are supported by upstream radar imagery with the \nidea of a complex of showers/thunderstorms congealing to our \nnorthwest over MI and OH and riding an advancing warm front \nsoutheast into and across our CWA late tonight into Tuesday \nmorning. The presence of the warm front and increasing \nsouthwesterly flow should continue to pull in moisture in the \nlow levels with a source region where surface dewpoints are \ncurrently around 80. Wind shear also increases overnight. So \nwhile timing suggest convection should be predominantly \nelevated, we cannot rule out some isolated higher wind gusts \nperhaps reaching the surface.\n\nProbably a bit more concerning given recent events is the threat\nfor locally heavy rainfall and flash flooding. PWATs are shown\nto rise over 2 inches up the Ohio River later this morning and\nHREF points to a potential for training of convection and an\nelevated risk of excessive rainfall in a narrow corridor \ndropping down over the heart of our CWA towards dawn. This looks\nlike a localized threat and I am not confident enough on the\nplacement of the area of heavy rain for a flood watch at this \ntime, but will definitely continue to monitor the potential as \nthe upstream convection takes shape through the predawn hours.\n\nAfter a lull in activity later in the morning, we should have \nseveral hours to destabilize in advance of the front, which will\npass southeast of the Ohio River during the early afternoon. We\nanticipate scattered to numerous showers/thunderstorms to \ndevelop in advance of the front and slide southeast with a \npotential for the strongest storms to carry a risk of damaging \nwinds and perhaps some hail, especially if the sun comes out for\nseveral hours. \n\nShowers and thunderstorms diminish quickly across our southeast\ncounties during the evening, but we remain close enough to a low\npressure system over NY and PA for a few showers or storms to \nfire in our northeast mountains Wednesday afternoon.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nHigh pressure builds over the Ohio and Tennessee Valleys from \nWednesday through Friday bringing pleasant weather. Models then \nshow a trough aloft digging in to our west over the weekend and \nperhaps cutting off from the mean flow into early next week. \nThis results in moisture advection and increasing chances for \nshowers/storms Saturday into Sunday, and lingering \nshowers/storms through at least Monday and probably into \nTuesday.",
